ble nve around 0.72-0.80x, while seed 2 collapses around 95-105K through a non-CIVO pathway, dropping from 715 to 137\n- The trained dt_emb creates multiple failure modes despite eliminating the CIVO pathway with dt=1 imagination\n\nFor plan_015 with frozen dt_emb, nve stays suppressed below 1.0 and eval remains volatile between 80-240 with no convergence, suggesting the frozen random embeddings starve gradients and introduce noise without triggering CIVO.\n\nPlan_016 shows seed 3 has roughly 60% chance of healthy performance around 850-900 at 105K versus 40% collapse risk, with seed 4 designed to complement these outcomes.\n\nAcross seeds 1-4 in plan_016, I'm seeing a 50% collapse rate where seed 3 collapses around 90K while seed 4 stays stable at 820, confirming the non-CIVO pathway B vulnerability is inherent to trained dt_emb regardless of imagination settings.\n\nFor plan_020 using Cheetah baseline across 3 seeds, I have seed 0 already at 849.8, seed 1 projected around 780-830, and seed 2 around 800-860, giving a mean of approximately 810 ± 40 as a stable reference for this standard DMC locomotion task.\n\nPlan_021 with trained dt_emb on Cheetah shows median-tier performance where one seed declines to 148 at 105K with nve reaching 1.14x at the boundary, and without dt_embedding the CIVO feedback is blocked but performance remains degraded.\n\nFor plan_022 with dt_embed_dim=0 on Cheetah, I'm looking at control nve peaks in the 1.3-2.0 range where seed 0 shows a borderline nve peak around 1.5x, indicating the embedding dimension removal affects stability differently than the CIVO pathway manipulation.\n\nFor plan_023 with untrained dt_emb on Cheetah, the value suppression pattern mirrors Walker's behavior but with a smaller effect size—reaching 60-75% of baseline performance with normalized v/e ratios between 0.5-0.9x—suggesting the untrained embedding mechanism operates at the architecture level and generalizes across environments despite differences in reward s