ient computational budget. Plan_052 is blocked on plan_050 anyway, so the paper will proceed with just the Walker and Cheetah results.\n\nFor plan_060, the uncertainty-gated truncation approach shows some promise as a P1 pilot—it reduces the collapse rate from 50% down to around 25% and gets evaluation performance to 70-80% of baseline—but the results aren't conclusive yet. The mechanism addresses the CIVO pathway but leaves Pathway B untouched, so more seeds would help validate whether this is a real solution or just noise.\n\nPlan_040 on Hopper Hop is marked P2 and won't run since a third environment would be a luxury we can't afford before the deadline.\n\nNow I'm structuring the full YAML output with the proper fields for each experiment—name, plan_id, description, expected results with metrics, rationale, and space for tables and figures.\n\nI need to be careful about the 008f_s3 borderline case that's in the registry but not assigned to any plan yet. I'll reference its expected behavior through plan_034's threshold analysis predictions rather than creating a separate entry.\n\nGenerating the comprehensive output... I'm scanning through the experiment registry to identify which plans are actually marked as planned versus completed. The first ten are done, but starting from plan_011 onward, I have a long list of planned experiments—including some that are cancelled like plan_024 and plan_053. I need to make sure I cover all the active planned ones in my YAML output. Now I'm cross-referencing these plans against the registry to see which ones already have results—looks like about half have existing data I can build from, while the others are completely new. Since I have actual outcomes for some of these experiments, I should make sure my predictions align with what actually happened rather than contradicting the evidence.\n\nLet me organize these by experiment type to make the output clearer: the Walker Walk variants, the cross-environment runs for Cheetah and Ca